From 30e2bf044e2daf5511f45e9beec5fe0cddf5df7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 10:03:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 28/40] lib/streamaggr: hand a series only to the aggregators
 that can match its name

Aggregators.Push hands every batch to every aggregator, and each runs its
match on every series. With the 102 rules of the ACG config, 100 of which
pin one metric name, every sample was matched 102 times; in a full-scale
replay of three targets the loop and the matches took 24% of vmagent's CPU,
nearly all of it on matches that could not succeed.

promrelabel.IfExpression.MetricNames returns the names an expression can
match when every filter group pins __name__ to a non-empty literal, as
MetricsQL applies a name to every group of an `or`; otherwise nil. At load
the aggregators are indexed by these names. Push hands the whole batch to
the aggregators that cannot be indexed and, to the others, only the runs
of series with their names: one run is pushed in place, several are copied
into a sub-batch whose matchIdxs are OR-ed back. A name is looked up once
per run, and the last few are remembered, as the buckets, sum and count of
a histogram take turns. Every aggregator still runs its full match.

Nothing else changes: outputs, matchIdxs and samples lag are the same, as
aggregators spared a push record a lag of 0 as they did. Push reads the
aggregators once and pushes nothing once they are stopped, which happens
on a config reload while pushes may be in flight. With fewer than two
aggregators to index there is no index.

Outputs and matchIdxs were byte for byte the same with and without the
index in a full-scale replay of 145 minutes (1.98 billion input series),
and in a sampled one with and without sample_timestamps. In the full-scale
replay vmagent took 0.82 cores instead of 1.30 and Aggregators.Push 63%
less time. Where the index skips nothing, with two or three indexed rules
whose names make up nearly every batch, reading the names costs about 5ns
a series and 50ns a push.
